# Phase 086 — Make `xs.map(f)` work when the receiver is a variable

## What changed

The fix landed in commit `a721434` ("resume phase-086: adopt preserved work"), which is
already merged into this tree; this resume run added no production change. It added
one entry to the acknowledgement list in `tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs` (the
resume commit had to be acknowledged for `cargo test` to be green) and this report.

| File | Lines | What |
|---|---|---|
| src/stdlib.rs | +26 −1 | new `pub fn map_function(args: &[Value]) -> Result<&FunctionValue, String>` (src/stdlib.rs:206) — the single place both engines get the callee for a method-spelled `map`, and the single place the bad-argument message is worded |
| src/interpreter.rs | +29 −13 | `Vm::call_method` asks the variable receiver's **value** for `map` after the module path has answered nothing (src/interpreter.rs:2141-2154); the object-receiver path now shares `stdlib::map_function` (src/interpreter.rs:2081) instead of an inline match |
| src/bytecode/vm.rs | +24 −6 | the same fallback in `BytecodeVm::call_method` (src/bytecode/vm.rs:2845-2858), on the same side of the module check; the object-receiver path shares the same helper (src/bytecode/vm.rs:2782) |
| tests/method_map_test.rs | +558 −0 | new file, 13 `#[test]`s, every case asked on **both** engines |
| tests/differential_test.rs | +22 −2 | `edge_a_higher_order_call_answers_the_same_way_on_both_engines` flipped its name-receiver case from "still refused" to asserting the answer, and gained a module-collision case and a number-receiver case |
| tests/doc_blocks_known_fail.txt | +2 −3 | `SPEC.md:647` moved out of the known-fail list (it was `RuntimeError: Module 'numbers' has no function 'map'`) |
| tests/doc_blocks_runs.txt | +3 −2 | `SPEC.md:647` moved into the runs list; counts 15 → 14 known failures, 48 → 49 running blocks |
| tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs | +1 −0 | this resume run: acknowledges `a721434` so `unreviewed_work_in_head_history_is_acknowledged` passes |

The receiver path is `object → module → list`. The list step is a **fallback**, asked
only after the module step has answered nothing, and only for `map` and only for a
name holding a `Value::List`.

## Reproduction (before the fix, as reported in the finding)

```
$ rb run spec647.rb          # set doubled to numbers.map(double)  -- SPEC.md:647
Error: RuntimeError: Module 'numbers' has no function 'map'
```

Both engines refused; `[1, 2, 3].map(f)` printed `[2, 4, 6]` throughout, which is why
the gap survived.

## Verification after the fix, run by hand on the built binary

```
$ rb run ./target/tmp/p086/spec647.rb          # SPEC.md:647 verbatim
[2, 4, 6]

$ rb compile ./target/tmp/p086/block.rb -o block.rbc && rb vm block.rbc
Compiled block.rb -> block.rbc
[2, 4, 6]

$ rb run ./target/tmp/p086/spellings.rb
[2, 4, 6]      # xs.map(double)
[2, 4, 6]      # list.map(xs, double)
[2, 4, 6]      # [1, 2, 3].map(double)
[2, 4, 6]      # map(xs, double)
$ rb compile ./target/tmp/p086/spellings.rb -o spellings.rbc && rb vm spellings.rbc
[2, 4, 6] / [2, 4, 6] / [2, 4, 6] / [2, 4, 6]

$ rb run ./target/tmp/p086/badargs.rb ; rb vm <compiled>
RuntimeError: map requires a function, but was given 5        (both engines, exit 1)
$ rb run ./target/tmp/p086/noargs.rb  ; rb vm <compiled>
RuntimeError: map requires a function, but no argument was given  (both engines, exit 1)
```

The one DoD line **not** met, stated plainly: `rb compile` + `rb vm` on SPEC.md:647
*verbatim* does not run, because `rb compile` refuses a function **literal**
(`set double to to (x) give back x * 2`) with

```
ParserError: `to (x) ... end` as an expression is not compiled yet; run programs that use one with `rb run`
```

That is a pre-existing bytecode-compiler limitation, untouched by this phase (the diff
changes only `call_method`); it is not caused by, and not in scope of, this fix. The
compiled half of the check is run on the **block form** of the same declaration, which
is the other of the two spellings SPEC.md gives for the same value and which does
compile. Tracked in `FINDINGS.md`.

## Tests added

`tests/method_map_test.rs`, 13 `#[test]`s, 11 of them `edge_*`. Each runs the program
on the tree-walking VM **and** on the bytecode VM and asserts the two answers are the
same `Value` — or the same error message.

| Test | Edge class covered |
|---|---|
| `edge_the_specification_example_of_a_first_class_function_runs` | the finding itself: SPEC.md:647, literal form on the tree-walker, block form compiled |
| `edge_every_spelling_of_map_answers_the_same_list` | four spellings of `map` each asserted against the literal `[2, 4, 6]` |
| `edge_a_variable_receiver_map_nests_and_recurses_within_the_call_limit` | nesting + recursion + resource: `map` nested inside the function it maps, and a `map` that recurses to `MAX_CALL_DEPTH` — the bytecode VM must not push a *host* frame per element |
| `edge_a_variable_receiver_holding_unicode_text_maps_element_by_element` | unicode: combining marks, CJK, RTL and an emoji, in a list of **text**, with a non-ASCII receiver name |
| `edge_a_list_of_records_maps_and_a_missing_field_is_a_runtime_error` | duplicate/missing keys + type mismatch: a list of records, and a record missing the field the function reads (`nothing * 2` is a clean RuntimeError, not a panic) |
| `edge_a_list_in_a_record_field_and_one_from_a_call_both_answer` | nesting: the list one dereference away, behind a record field read and behind a call |
| `edge_an_empty_and_a_singleton_receiver_answer` | empty + singleton; the function in the empty case divides by zero, so an implementation that called the callee for an empty list fails instead of printing `[]` |
| `edge_a_receiver_named_after_a_module_maps_the_list_it_holds` | module-name collision in both directions: a module with no `map` member (`text`, `files`) reaches the fallback; a module **with** one (`list` → `list_map`) keeps the module answer and stays refused on arity |
| `a_module_receiver_is_not_shadowed_by_the_list_rule` | regression: `text.uppercase("hi")` → `HI` and `files.exists(...)` → `false` with a list bound to those very names; fails if the fallback runs before the module check |
| `an_object_receiver_still_dispatches` | regression: `object`/`has`/`to` method dispatch with `this` bound; fails if the list fallback shadows the object step |
| `edge_a_receiver_whose_value_is_nothing_is_refused` | empty/"nothing": `nothing` is not a list, so the receiver still gets `Module 'xs' has no function 'map'` on both engines |
| `edge_a_bad_argument_to_a_variable_receiver_map_is_a_named_runtime_error` | type mismatch + failure assertion: `xs.map(5)` names the `5`; `xs.map()` says no argument was given |
| `edge_a_receiver_that_is_not_a_list_is_still_refused` | type mismatch: a number, a text and a record receiver keep the pre-existing receiver refusal rather than reaching the argument check |

Plus `tests/differential_test.rs::edge_a_higher_order_call_answers_the_same_way_on_both_engines`,
extended from 1 to 3 name-receiver cases.

Two pre-existing test lists moved as a direct consequence of the behaviour change, not as
a weakening: `SPEC.md:647` leaves `tests/doc_blocks_known_fail.txt` (which exists to name
known failures) and joins `tests/doc_blocks_runs.txt`. Both files' own header counts were
